S=[
("hook","Pull into a tight parking space, and your car does something that looks impossible. On the screen, there it is: your car, seen from directly above, with the lines of the parking bay all around it."),
("hook2","But there's one problem. There's no camera up there. No pole, no drone, nothing floating over the roof. So how is your car showing you a view from a place where no camera exists?"),
("title","The answer involves four cameras, some clever geometry, and a computer quietly rebuilding the world around you, many times every second."),
("cams","Let's start with the hardware. Most surround-view systems use four cameras. One sits at the front, usually in or near the grille or badge. One is at the rear, often near the number plate or tailgate. And one is tucked under each side mirror, looking down at the ground."),
("note","Not every car does it exactly this way. Some vehicles add extra cameras, and positions vary between manufacturers. But four cameras, one on each side, is the classic layout."),
("fisheye","Each of these cameras has an extremely wide-angle lens, often called a fisheye lens. Many can take in a field of view of around 180 degrees. That's what lets just four cameras cover a full circle around the car."),
("bend","But there's a catch. To squeeze that much of the world into one picture, a fisheye lens bends it. Straight lines curve. Things at the edges get squashed. Parking lines look like they're melting."),
("raw","So the raw footage from these four cameras looks nothing like a top-down view. It's four warped images, each looking out in a different direction. The trick is turning them into one picture."),
("undistort","Step one is undoing the distortion. Engineers measure exactly how each lens bends light, and the system uses that model to straighten the image back out, pixel by pixel."),
("homography","Step two is the clever part: changing the point of view. The system assumes the ground around the car is flat. If you know exactly where a camera is, and which way it's pointing, you can calculate where every point on that flat ground would appear if you were looking straight down at it. Mathematicians call this kind of mapping a homography."),
("reproject","So each camera's image is reprojected, as if it had been taken from above. Front, rear, left and right become four slices of ground, each seen from a viewpoint that doesn't really exist."),
("stitch","Step three is stitching. Neighbouring cameras overlap at the corners, so the system blends those regions together and evens out differences in brightness and colour, to make the seams harder to see."),
("overlay","And the car in the middle? That isn't a photo of your car at all. The cameras can't see your roof. It's a graphic, placed into the empty space in the centre. The whole picture is a composite, generated in real time."),
("calib","All of this depends on one thing: the system knowing precisely where each camera is. Shift a camera by a small amount, or tilt it by a fraction of a degree, and the four slices no longer line up."),
("target","That's why surround-view systems are calibrated. Typically, patterned targets, often checkerboards, are laid on the floor where neighbouring cameras overlap. Because the system knows exactly where those patterns should be, it can work out each camera's real position and angle."),
("recal","And if a mirror is replaced, a bumper is repaired, or a camera gets knocked, the system may need recalibrating. Skip that, and the view can show lines that don't meet, or a kerb that seems to bend at the corners."),
("limits","Which brings us to what the system can't do. Remember the flat ground assumption? It works well for painted lines. But anything that sticks up, like a pole, a bollard, or a person, breaks it."),
("stretch","Tall objects get smeared outward, stretched as if they were lying flat on the ground. Near the seams between cameras, an object can look distorted, appear doubled, or partly vanish."),
("blind","There can also be blind spots, especially very close to the car's body, and a camera can be hampered by dirt, rain, or low sun. What you see is a calculated reconstruction, not a live view from above."),
("park","That's why car manufacturers warn drivers not to rely on the screen alone. It's a parking aid, not a replacement for mirrors, sensors, and actually turning your head."),
("summary","So let's put it all together. Four wide-angle cameras capture the world around the car. Software corrects their distortion, reprojects each image onto a flat virtual ground, and stitches them into one picture, with an image of your car placed in the middle."),
("end","So next time your car shows you a bird's-eye view, remember: there's no camera floating above your car. Just four cameras, some clever mathematics, and a computer doing a ridiculous amount of work to help you park."),
("cta","If this changed how you see that little screen, subscribe for more of the hidden science behind everyday things. And tell us in the comments: what everyday technology should we take apart next? This has been The Unnecessary Lab."),
]
